= Raisins (dried Vitis vinifera)

_Scope: This entry treats the dried grape — the fruit of Vitis vinifera, sun- or shade-dried into a raisin — as its primary referent, treating the fresh grape by contrast where a tradition distinguishes the two (Ayurveda and Tibb both grade fresh vs. dried, seeded vs. seedless). Named forms recur: English raisin (large, usually seeded), sultana (seedless golden), and currant (the tiny Zante/Corinth grape); Ayurvedic munakkā (large seeded) vs. kishmish (small seedless); Arabic zabīb. Status: fully classical in every system — Chinese (葡萄乾), Ayurvedic (द्राक्षा drākṣā, ranked phalottamā, "best of fruits"), Tibb (زبيب zabīb, with strong Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation), and Greco-Roman (Dioscorides' σταφίς, the Roman uva passa)._

*Relation to parent food:* raisin ← dried grape. Drying concentrates the sugars and shifts the classical reading — the fresh grape is cooling-and-juicy, the raisin heavier, sweeter, warmer-tending, and mildly laxative — so the traditions treat them as related but graded drugs, not identical.

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Vitis vinifera_ L. (family Vitaceae); _vītis_ = "vine," _vīnifera_ = "wine-bearing." The dried fruit is *uva passa* (pl. _uvae passae_), "spread-out grape," from _passus_ (past participle of _pandere_, "to spread out" — i.e. spread in the sun to dry); the officinal pharmacy name is _Uvae passae_.#footnote[_Vītis_, _uva_, and _uva passa_ (< _passus_, "spread out to dry"): Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_; de Vaan, _Etymological Dictionary of Latin_ (s.v. _pando_). The pharmacy term _Uvae passae_ for officinal raisins: standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_).]

*English:* _Raisin_ — from Old French _raisin_, "grape," from Vulgar Latin _racīmus_ / Latin _racēmus_, "cluster of grapes" (whence also English _raceme_). English *narrowed* the borrowed word to the *dried* grape while keeping native "grape" for the fresh. *Currant* is a contraction of "raisins of *Corinth*" (the tiny Zante/Corinthian grape); *sultana* names the seedless golden raisin (from the "Sultana"/Thompson-seedless grape) — three English words for graded dried-grape forms.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "raisin, n.", "currant, n.", "sultana, n."): _raisin_ < Old French < Latin _racēmus_ "cluster"; _currant_ < "raisins of Corinth"; _sultana_ from the grape variety. The narrowing of "raisin" (originally "grape") to the dried fruit is an English development.]

*Chinese:* *葡萄 (pútáo)* = grape; *葡萄乾 (pútáo gān)* = dried grape / raisin (乾 gān = dried). _Pútáo_ is a *Han-period loanword* from the Western Regions (西域), brought — with the vine itself — along the route opened by the Zhang Qian (張騫) embassy; the word is generally traced to an Iranian or Hellenistic-Bactrian source (cf. Greek _bótrys_ "cluster"), marking the grape as an Iranian/Silk-Road introduction to China.#footnote[葡萄 as a Han loanword accompanying the Western-Regions introduction of the vine (Zhang Qian, d. 113 BCE): recorded in _Shi Ji_ (史記) and _Han Shu_ (漢書); the foreign etymology (Iranian/Bactrian, cf. Gk. _bótrys_) is discussed in Berthold Laufer, _Sino-Iranica_ (1919). See the 西域 / 胡-prefix glossary notes.]

*Sanskrit:* *द्राक्षा (drākṣā)* — grape/raisin, one of the most-praised classical fruits; the celebrated dictum *द्राक्षा फलोत्तमा (drākṣā phalottamā)*, "the grape is the best of fruits." Synonyms: *मृद्वीका (mṛdvīkā, "the tender/soft one")*, *गोस्तनी (gostanī, "cow-udder,"* for the cluster shape), *स्वादुफला (svāduphalā, "sweet-fruited")*. The Persian-derived dried-fruit names *मुनक्का (munakkā,* large seeded raisin) and *किशमिश (kishmish,* small seedless) are standard in Indian pharmacy usage.#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _drākṣā_, _mṛdvīkā_, _gostanī_); the _drākṣā phalottamā_ dictum and the dravya treatment: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Āmrādi-phala-varga_. _Munakkā_ / _kishmish_ are Persian-derived pharmacy terms in wide Ayurvedic use.]

*Arabic / Persian / Urdu (Tibb):* *عنب (ʿinab)* = grape; *زبيب (zabīb)* = raisin (the seedless deseeded raisin is *منقّى, munaqqā*, "cleaned"). Persian: *انگور (angūr)* = grape, *کشمش (kishmish)* / *مویز (mavīz)* = raisin. Urdu/Hakeemi: *مُنقّٰی (munaqqā)* and *کشمش (kishmish)*. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.#footnote[_ʿinab_ (grape) and _zabīb_ (raisin): Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II (_zabīb_, _ʿinab_); Persian _angūr_/_kishmish_/_mavīz_ standard. The Qurʾānic grape/vineyard register (_aʿnāb_) is treated in the Tibb section below.]

*Greek:* *σταφίς (staphís)* = raisin; *σταφυλή (staphylḗ)* = grape-cluster; *βότρυς (bótrys)* = cluster; *ἄμπελος (ámpelos)* = vine; *οἶνος (oînos)* = wine.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

Species: _V. vinifera_. Grape (葡萄) is listed in the *superior class (上品)* of the _Shennong Bencao Jing_ — the class of longevity/tonic drugs — where prolonged use is said to "lighten the body and extend the years," a rasāyana-like framing.

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Neutral (平); the dried raisin tends slightly warm.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘 gān) and sour (酸 suān).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Lung (肺), Spleen (脾), Kidney (腎).#footnote[Grape in the _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (神農本草經) superior class (上品) with the "lighten the body / extend the years" formula; property, flavor, channels: Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 葡萄 entry; systematized in Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Actions & indications:*
- *補氣血 (bǔ qì xuè)* — tonifies qi and blood; for deficiency with fatigue, pallor, palpitations.
- *益肝腎、強筋骨 (yì gān shèn, qiáng jīn gǔ)* — augments Liver and Kidney and strengthens sinews and bones; for weak/aching lower back and knees and wind-damp _bì_ (rheumatic) pain.
- *利小便 (lì xiǎo biàn)* — promotes urination; for edema and dysuria.
- *安胎 (ān tāi)* — calms the fetus; for restless-fetus with qi-blood weakness.

*Cautions:* the sweet-moist nature can generate damp-heat in excess; the concentrated sugar of the raisin is a modern caution in diabetes (消渴-adjacent).#footnote[Actions (qi-blood and Liver-Kidney tonic, diuretic, fetus-calming): _Bencao Gangmu_ 葡萄 entry; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The diabetes/sugar caution is a modern (M) overlay on the classical sweet-damp warning.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Species: _V. vinifera_ (द्राक्षा / मृद्वीका). The archetypal *madhura–śīta–snigdha* fruit and a leading _rasāyana_/_vṛṣya_ dravya; classically *phalottama*, "the best of fruits."

*Rasa (taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet); a faint _amla_ (sour) in some grapes/fresh fruit.

*Guṇa (qualities):* Guru (गुरु, heavy), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous); the dried _munakkā_ is heavier and more laxative, the fresh fruit lighter and juicier.

*Vīrya (potency):* Śīta (शीत, cooling).

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of drākṣā and the fresh/dried grading: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Āmrādi-phala-varga_; P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II. The _phalottama_ ranking is the standard classical praise.]

*Prabhāva (specific action):* No distinct prabhāva is classically assigned — drākṣā's actions (the premier _raktapitta-hara_ and _vṛṣya-rasāyana_ fruit, and its gentle laxative effect) all follow from the madhura–śīta–snigdha–guru profile. Its *phalottama* status is an evaluative epithet, not a prabhāva-level claim.

*Doṣa effect:* Vāta-Pitta-śāmaka (the sweet-cooling-unctuous profile pacifies both — a rare doubly-pacifying fruit); increases Kapha in excess.

*Karma (actions):*
- _Vṛṣya, Rasāyana, Balya, Bṛṃhaṇa_ — aphrodisiac, rejuvenative, strengthening, nourishing (for _kṣaya_/wasting and _daurbalya_/debility).
- _Raktapittahara, Dāhahara, Tṛṣṇāhara_ — relieves bleeding-Pitta, burning, and thirst (the cooling actions).
- _Kāsahara, Śvāsahara, Kaṇṭhya_ — relieves cough and dyspnea, benefits the throat/voice.
- _Mṛdu-virecana / Anulomana_ — gentle laxative (especially soaked _munakkā_ for constipation).
- _Hṛdya, Jvarahara, Mūtrala_ — cardiac-benefiting, antipyretic (drākṣā in _jvara_), mild diuretic.#footnote[Karma compiled from Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Āmrādi-phala-varga_, and Caraka/Suśruta usage (drākṣā in _raktapitta_, _jvara_, _kṣaya_, and the _madhura-skandha_); P. V. Sharma, vol. II. The soaked-_munakkā_ laxative use is standard clinical practice.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S core saṃhitā, N later nighaṇṭu, F folk, M modern/pharmacological)_

- *(N) Conditional:* Kapha excess, _prameha_ (the diabetes/urinary-sugar cluster), obesity (_medoroga_) — the madhura-guru-snigdha nature aggravates these.
- *(F) Phala-with-milk caveat:* the general folk rule against fruit-with-milk (_phala + dugdha_) is *waived* for drākṣā — _drākṣā-kṣīrapāka_ (raisins simmered in milk) is a classical Pitta-pacifying, laxative, brṃhaṇa preparation, not a viruddha.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:* the concentrated fructose-glucose load of raisins is a glycemic caution in diabetes; sulfited golden raisins/sultanas can trigger *sulfite-sensitive asthma*; note also the food-safety issue of *ochratoxin A* in poorly-dried vine fruit.#footnote[The prameha/kapha caution and the drākṣā-kṣīra exception: Bhāvaprakāśa and standard dravyaguṇa. The (M) glycemic, sulfite-asthma, and ochratoxin-A points are modern food-science overlays — flagged as non-classical; verify against current food-safety literature.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Grape (*عنب ʿinab*) and raisin (*زبيب zabīb*) are fully canonical in Tibb — a leading *hepatic tonic, nutritive, and gentle laxative* — and carry unusually strong *Ṭibb al-Nabawī* attestation. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.

*Arabic:* *عنب (ʿinab)* grape, *زبيب (zabīb)* raisin, *منقّى (munaqqā)* deseeded raisin. Persian: *انگور (angūr)*, *کشمش (kishmish)*, *مویز (mavīz)*. Urdu/Hakeemi: *مُنقّٰی*, *کشمش*.

*Mizāj:* *Hot and Moist in the 1st degree* for the sweet raisin (Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II); the seeds are more astringent-drying, the flesh moist-laxative.#footnote[Mizāj and actions of _zabīb_/_ʿinab_: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II (_zabīb_, _ʿinab_); Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_. The Hot-Moist 1st-degree placement is the standard Avicennan reading for the sweet raisin.]

*Afʿāl (actions):*
- *Muqawwī al-kabid* — liver tonic (the signature Tibb action of the raisin); *mufattiḥ li-suddad al-kabid wa-l-ṭiḥāl* — deobstruent of hepatic and splenic congestion.
- *Muqawwī al-maʿida* and *muqawwī al-badan* — stomach and general body tonic; *mughadhdhī* — nutritive (a genuine food-medicine).
- *Mulayyin* — gentle laxative (the fleshy raisin); *munaḍḍij / munfith li-l-ṣadr* — concoctive/expectorant for chest and cough (raisin decoction).
- The *seeds* are *qābiḍ* (astringent) — hence deseeding (_munaqqā_) for the laxative use.

*Istemālāt (indications):* hepatic and splenic weakness and obstruction; convalescence and debility; chest complaints and cough (raisin decoction); constipation.

*Mawāneʿ / Iḥtirāzāt (contraindications):* hot bilious excess in quantity; the sugar load in diabetes/obesity (modern overlay).

*Muṣliḥ (correctives):* a sour agent (pomegranate _rummān_) or deseeding, to temper the sweet-moist heaviness. *Badal (substitutes):* dates (_tamr_), figs (_tīn_).

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *strong.* Grapes and vineyards (*أعناب aʿnāb*) recur through the Qurʾān as a wholesome and paradisal fruit (e.g. 2:266; 6:99; 13:4; 16:11, 67; 78:32). The raisin specifically appears in the Prophetic dietary tradition as *نبيذ الزبيب (nabīdh al-zabīb)*, "raisin-water" — raisins steeped overnight in water to make a sweet, non-intoxicating infusion, reported among the Prophet's drinks (and to be discarded before it ferments to intoxication).#footnote[Qurʾānic _aʿnāb_ (grapes/vineyards): 2:266; 6:99; 13:4; 16:11, 67; 78:32, among others. _Nabīdh al-zabīb_ (raisin-infusion) in the hadith of the Prophet's drink, with the non-intoxication proviso: Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim, _Kitāb al-Ashriba_; Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya, _al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī_ (_zabīb_). The prohibition attaches once it ferments to _khamr_.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *葡萄酒 (pútáo jiǔ, grape wine)* — grape wine entered China from the Western Regions and became famous by the Tang; used as a warming qi-blood tonic drink in small measure.
- *Grape/raisin in tonic diet* — 葡萄乾 in congee (粥), tonic soups, and qi-blood-building convalescent food; the food-medicine (食療) register rather than a decoction-herb.
- *In Kidney/sinew tonic contexts* — paired with tonics for wind-damp _bì_ and lumbar weakness (the 益肝腎強筋骨 action).#footnote[Grape wine's Western-Regions origin and Tang popularity, and the food-therapy (食療) register of raisins: _Bencao Gangmu_ 葡萄 entry; standard Chinese dietetics (食療) literature. Grape is a food-tonic more than a formula-herb.]

=== Ayurveda

- *द्राक्षासव / द्राक्षारिष्ट (Drākṣāsava / Drākṣāriṣṭa)* — the classical *fermented grape tonic* (an _āsava/ariṣṭa_), for debility, cough, dyspnea, hepatic and cardiac weakness, anemia, and as a digestive-and-appetizer; one of the most-used classical liquid tonics.
- *द्राक्षादि क्वाथ / Mṛdvīkādi (Drākṣādi kwātha)* — decoctions with drākṣā as chief, for Pitta, _raktapitta_, fever, and thirst.
- *Drākṣā-kṣīrapāka* — raisins simmered in milk, for Pitta, constipation, and _brṃhaṇa_ nourishment.
- *In Cyavanaprāśa and rasāyana avalehas* — drākṣā is a standard sweet-cooling nourishing ingredient; soaked _munakkā_ (with black pepper for cough, or alone for constipation) is everyday household practice.#footnote[Drākṣāsava/Drākṣāriṣṭa and Drākṣādi preparations: _Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_ and the _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_; drākṣā in Cyavanaprāśa per the classical Cyavanaprāśa recipe (Caraka Cikitsāsthāna 1). Household munakkā use is standard clinical practice.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Greco-Roman:* *Dioscorides* (_De Materia Medica_ V on vine/grape/raisin; σταφίς) treats raisins for the mouth and gums, throat and cough, and the kidneys and bladder, and grape/wine extensively; *Pliny* (_Naturalis Historia_ XIV, XXIII) on the vine, _uva passa_, and the raisin-wines. The Romans made *passum* (sweet raisin-wine) and *defrutum* (grape-must syrup) as both food and medicine.
- *Officinal pharmacy:* *Uvae passae* were long officinal — a *demulcent and sweetening excipient* and one of the *"pectoral fruits"* (with jujube, fig, and date) in cough decoctions and electuaries; raisins masked bitter drugs and softened the chest.
- *Modern survivals:* raisins persist as a food and a folk remedy (e.g. the fringe "gin-soaked raisins" for arthritis — flagged as folk, not evidenced); grape-derived *resveratrol* and grape-seed polyphenols are a large modern-nutraceutical register (the "French paradox"), with mostly modest human evidence.#footnote[Greco-Roman: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ (Beck translation); Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XIV, XXIII (Loeb). _Passum_/_defrutum_ and _Uvae passae_ as pectoral/demulcent officinals: standard food- and pharmacy-history (Dalby, _Food in the Ancient World_; Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_). Resveratrol/French-paradox claims are modest in human evidence — flagged.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

Covered above: *zabīb* as hepatic tonic, nutritive, gentle laxative, and chest-concoctive; canonical in *sharāb* and *maʿjūn* preparations (raisins as a sweet base/excipient), in raisin decoction (*ماء الزبيب, māʾ al-zabīb*), and in the Prophetic *nabīdh al-zabīb*.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

The grapevine is the great *warm-temperate* fruit of the Mediterranean, the Near East, the Caucasus (its likely domestication homeland), and the continental Silk-Road oases; *raisin-making is a hot-dry-summer preservation technology* — sun- or shade-drying that turns a perishable cooling fruit into a concentrated, storable, warming winter-and-caravan food. See `climates.typ`.

=== Warm-dry Mediterranean & Near-Eastern / continental Silk-Road (the home)

Greek, Levantine, Persian, and Central-Asian dry-summer zones (Iran, Afghanistan, and the Turpan basin of Xinjiang — the great raisin regions). Constitutionally warm-dry summers carry Pitta and external 燥/熱; the *fresh grape* is the seasonal *cooling, juicy, raktapitta-pacifying, thirst-quenching* summer fruit, while the *raisin* is its *transposed winter form* — concentrated sweetness and warmth for the cold, dry season and for travel. *Cares.* The fresh fruit is apt in the summer heat (śīta, tṛṣṇāhara); the raisin's heavier-sweeter-warmer profile is apt in cold/dry seasons and for convalescence — eating them in the climatically apt season is the point.

=== Adoptive temperate and global

Grapes and raisins now grow and ship worldwide; the raisin's shelf-stability made it one of history's premier trade-and-travel foods. In cool-damp and continental zones the raisin is a winter energy-and-tonic food (baking, tonic wines, convalescent diet).

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_ / _kāla-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

The chief modern mismatch is *seasonal and dosal, not geographic*: the raisin is a *concentrated-sweet cold-season/convalescent* food, but the modern snack pattern eats fistfuls year-round, including in heat and in Kapha/_prameha_-prone constitutions — a _kāla-viruddha_ (wrong-season) and _mātrā_ (wrong-quantity) drift from the classical use of the fresh grape in summer and the raisin in measured, seasonal, or medicinal amounts. See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Raisins are a *whole food* — an energy-dense dried fruit; the relevant figures are dietary, not toxicological.

*Composition (per 100 g raisins):*
- Energy ~300 kcal; water ~15 g.
- *Sugars ~59–72 g* (roughly equal glucose and fructose) — the defining feature; carbohydrate ~79 g, *fiber ~3.7 g*.
- Protein ~3 g; negligible fat.
- Minerals: *potassium ~750 mg* (high), iron ~1.9 mg, some calcium, magnesium, copper, manganese, boron.
- *Polyphenols* — flavonols (quercetin), phenolic acids, and small amounts of *resveratrol* (mostly in the skin/seed and far more in wine than raisins); *tartaric acid* (grape-specific).#footnote[Composition: USDA FoodData Central (raisins, seedless). The near-equal glucose/fructose, high potassium, modest fiber, and grape-specific tartaric acid are characteristic; resveratrol in raisins is small relative to red wine — verify current figures.]

*Pharmacology / physiology of note:*
- *Glycemic profile* — high sugar but a moderate glycemic index/load (fiber, tartaric acid, and the fructose fraction temper the response); studies show raisins compare reasonably to other snacks for post-prandial glucose, though the sugar density remains a real caution in diabetes.
- *Cardiovascular* — modest evidence for small blood-pressure and lipid benefits (potassium, polyphenols, fiber); often overstated.
- *Laxative* — fiber plus tartaric acid and sorbitol give a genuine mild laxative effect (the classical _mṛdu-virecana_/_mulayyin_ action).
- *Dental* — sticky and sugary (a caries caution), though raisin phenolics (oleanolic acid) have some anti-cariogenic antibacterial activity — a mixed picture.
- *Antioxidant* — meaningful polyphenol content; the grape/resveratrol "French paradox" story is *modest and much-hyped* in human data.

*Safe daily intake / practical figure:* no toxicological ceiling for a whole food; the limiting factors are *sugar and energy density*.
- *Practical portion:* ~*30–40 g (a small handful / ¼ cup)* is a sensible serving; a whole "mini-box" is ~14–28 g.
- *Risk groups / cautions:* diabetes and weight management (sugar/energy density); *sulfite-sensitive asthma* (golden raisins/sultanas are often sulfured); dental caries (sticky sugar); *ochratoxin-A* food-safety concern in poorly-dried vine fruit (buy well-processed).
- *Famous non-human hazard:* *grapes and raisins are acutely nephrotoxic to dogs* (idiosyncratic acute kidney injury) — a well-known veterinary caution, mechanism still uncertain; keep them away from dogs.
- *Reference/dietary information, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Glycemic, cardiovascular, laxative, and dental data: current nutrition literature — modest effects, flagged. Sulfite-asthma and ochratoxin-A: food-safety literature. Canine grape/raisin nephrotoxicity is well documented in veterinary toxicology (mechanism uncertain). These are dietary reference points, not medical advice; verify current figures.]

